[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Flexbox Architecture, Alignment, and Sizing Algorithms"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Flexible Box Layout Module, commonly known as Flexbox, is a one-dimensional layout model designed to distribute space dynamically and define powerful alignment capabilities. Understanding its core architecture, sizing algorithms, and axis orientation is essential for building responsive modern web applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Flexbox Architecture: Main and Cross Axes"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Flexbox layout relies heavily on directional geometry. Unlike block or inline formatting contexts, Flexbox operates along two perpendicular axes simultaneously: the main axis and the cross axis."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Main Axis: The primary axis along which flex items are laid out. Controlled via 'flex-direction'."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cross Axis: The axis perpendicular to the main axis. Cross-sizing and alignment depend on this axis."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":".container {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: row; /* or column, row-reverse, column-reverse */
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. The Sizing Algorithms: Flex-Grow, Flex-Shrink, and Flex-Basis"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The true power of Flexbox lies in its sizing algorithms, governed by the 'flex' shorthand property. The browser allocates free space or absorbs deficits using mathematical calculations based on three main properties."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"flex-basis: Defines the default size of an element before free space is distributed."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"flex-grow: Determines how much a flex item will expand relative to the rest of the flex items when positive free space is available."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"flex-shrink: Dictates how much a flex item will shrink when negative free space (overflow) occurs."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":".item {
  /* flex: grow shrink basis */
  flex: 1 1 200px;
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Alignment Mechanics"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Alignment properties control how items position themselves inside the flex container along both axes. 'justify-content' operates on the main axis, whereas 'align-items' manages cross-axis alignment for all items."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":".container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
  align-items: center;
  align-content: stretch;
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"By mastering the interplay between the main/cross axes, the mathematical sizing algorithms of flex-grow/shrink/basis, and directional alignment properties, you can construct predictable, robust layouts without relying on floats or complex positioning tricks."}]
